d. obtain decent work in accordance with her capabilities; e. obtain work leave while receiving wages/salary in accordance with statutory regulations;
f. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and pathways of education;
g. obtain special protection in the implementation of her work or profession against matters that could threaten her safety and or health in relation to women's reproductive function; and
h. ownership and management of joint assets in marriage in accordance with statutory regulations.

Article 61
(1) Preventive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 59 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing the occurrence of disease or health problems.
(2) The efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by:
a. obligating mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding to infants up to 6 (six) months of age;
b. providing free immunization and vitamins to infants and toddlers;
c. providing nutritious food to toddlers from underprivileged families;
d. optimizing the role of posyandu for health services for mothers and toddlers;
e. conducting periodic health examinations; f. providing sanitation facilities and clean water at every school; and
g. providing special breastfeeding facilities or breastfeeding corner rooms at government agencies or business entities.

Article 3
Women's Empowerment and Child Protection aims to: a. serve as a guideline for local government in planning, policies and strategies for the protection of women and children;
b. protect women and children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of women and children; c. prevent violations of the rights of women and children;
d. increase empowerment of women and children;
e. enhance the role and value of local wisdom and the role of customs in the protection of women and children; and f. enhance the role of government and non-government institutions in the region in efforts to protect women and children.

Article 12
(1) In order to improve the quality of life of women, the Regional Government is responsible for:
a. respecting and guaranteeing the rights of women without discrimination in accordance with human dignity and worth;
b. implementing Gender Mainstreaming in the Region; c. establishing a Gender Mainstreaming working group in the Region; d. enhancing the empowerment of Women in terms of role, quality and capacity in the fields of:
1. politics;
2. law;
3. social; and
4. economy.
e. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for women's empowerment in the region. (2) The responsibility of the regional government in efforts to improve the quality of life of women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by the relevant Regional Apparatus.

Article 2
(1) The implementation of protection for women is based on the principles of:
a. the best interests of women;
b. gender justice and gender equality; c. local wisdom; and
d. legal certainty.
(2) The implementation of protection for children is based on the principles of: a. non-discrimination;
b. the best interests of the child;
c. the right to life, survival, and development;
d. respect for the views of the child; and e. legal certainty.

Article 22
Efforts to increase the role, quality and capability of women in the field include:
a. implementing programs, activities, training for improving the skills and expertise of women in order to realize the improvement of women's welfare;
b. facilitation of business capital strengthening for women who have weak economies;
c. marketing of products resulting from the businesses of women who have weak economies;
d. training for developing women's capabilities through economic empowerment of pre-prosperous families;
e. entrepreneurship training for women comprehensively.
